  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   - The central idea is to improve upon the current upper bound for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by using a combination of global and local search strategies. The current algorithm uses greedy binary construction and SLSQP optimization, but this may get stuck in local optima.\n   - We will use a multi-stage approach: start with the provided parent construction if available, then apply a simulated annealing method to explore the continuous function space, followed by constrained optimization. This will help escape local optima and find better candidates.\n   - We iterate over multiple starting points (the parent and some periodic variations) to ensure diverse exploration. The key is to balance exploration (simulated annealing) and exploitation (constrained optimization) across different time budgets.\n   - The algorithm will be hierarchical: first use PSO for high-level exploration, then apply CVXPY for constrained convex quadrant \u2013 wait, no, the problem is not convex, so we use quadratic programming for trust region steps.\n   - The main bottleneck is the expensive strict evaluation via correlation which is O(n\u00b2), so we use surrogate objectives for early stages. We'll start with a low-ranked initial guess and progress to more accurate computations.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - Variables: h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for x \u2208 [0,2], represented discretely with n=samples at step dx=2/n.\n   - Objective: C5 = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx, with dx=2/n normalization factor (reduces dimension).\n   - Constraints: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641, sum(h[i]) = n/2 exactly.\n   - Search space is high-dimensional (n): 180 degrees of freedom.\n   - Secondary optimization: minimize the maximum overlap (C5) with respect to h.\n   - For surrogate implementation, we use:\n      $$$proxy(C) = \\sum_{k} \\exp(-\u03b2(k)(\\text{currOverlap} - C))$$$\n     This is a negative-log-sum-exp of the centered overlaps, amortizing computation.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   - Inputs: seed (42 by default), budget_s (1100 seconds)\n   - Output: best_h, best_C, n_points\n   - Phases:\n     1. Initialize candidates:\n        - If parent (initial_h_values) is provided, use it as a strong initial candidate.\n        - Generate diverse candidates via:\n            a) Random uniform samples with correct sum (periodic variation with periods [2,4,6]) \n            b) Golden section fractal-like gaps (low-discrepancy for non-periodic)\n        - Compute initial gaps using lightweight methods (direct correlation with small n for quick estimate)\n     2. Hierarchical optimization:\n        a) Unscented filter passes: \n            - Use PSO with population of 20: minimize surrogated C5 with different \u03b2's.\n            - Accept candidates with improved C5.\n            - Reduce population over time: start 50 agents, cull to 10 as budget nears.\n        b) Transition to exact evaluations:\n            - When time < 800s: use surrogated models with geometrically increasing accuracies.\n            - Use LBFGS-B for constrained minimization with projection onto feasible set (face-centered projection).\n            - Use barrier method for constraints: log barrier for [0,1] bounds and L1 projection for sum constraint.\n        c) Precomputations: \n            - Pre-split n automatically based on available budget and initial estimate of time-to-solution.\n            - Use linear interpolation between points for faster correlation-like approximations during surrogated evaluations.\n        - Feasibility restoration: Use exponential smoothing and cumulative sum adjustments with gradient descent-like steps.\n   - Helper functions must include:\n     - Rapid overlap calculation (avoid convolution when k exceeds dx space)\n     - Multi-start guaranteed: We maintain 5 best candidates at all times to avoid premature convergence\n     - Progressive evaluation: Initially compute only x, x\u00b11,... and extrapolate via interpolation for speed.\n\n4. Search Choices\n   - Primary iterations:\n     1. Rapid exploring PSO (first time available, choose time/speed tradeoffs)\n     2. Constrained gradient boosting (L-BFGS) from PSO centroids\n     3. Look-to-behind strategy: retrain models periodically with fresh population samples\n   - Key hyperparameters:\n     - Population size: 10 (constant) to balance diversity and computation.\n     - Temperature schedule: exponential decay until plateau.\n     - Step size for updates: randomized relative movement correction.\n   - Stop conditions: \n     - Maximum iterations without improvement: 300\n     - Elapsed time: 99% of budget\n\n5. Computation budget and robustness\n   - Dominant costs: \n     - Correlation: O(n) fine-grained + O(2n-1) correlations (2n-1 interrogation points)\n     - We restrict fine-grid work to adaptive steps based on improvement rate.\n   - Check deadlines inside evaluation loops: use time.sleep(0.1) for periodic checks.\n   - If a run exceeds time for calculations then reduce grid resolution locally.\n   - Fallback: Return parent solution wrapped in a Gaussian-smoothed version.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Feasibility checks:\n     - final_h: Poisson or log-normal distributed zeros if optimization fails.\n     - Check sum(f)\u2248n/2 (within \u221aepsilon * n)\n     - C5: finite and computed with mode=\"full\"\n   - Return format: \n     - [h1,...,hn] as list\n     - C5 bound as float representing the minimized max overlap\n     - n_points fixed to 360 by default, may use n=1000 during quick tests (not implemented).\n   - Correlation requirements: \n     - implement as discrete sum with dx=2.0/n.\n     - Must match previous implementation's topological boundaries.\n   - Small tests:\n     - Test n=0: all zeros, max_overlap=2.0/n.\n     - Test periodic ramp function: h(x)=sin(kx), normalize, compute C5.\n     - Validation reports: print(C5_reduction) every 100 iterations.",
